  <h2>I wanted to be a doctor. This was another way in.</h2>
  <p>
    As a kid I wanted to become a doctor, because doctors get to help people every day. I ended up in tech
    instead. This thesis is where the two meet: if an assistant can answer questions about a record reliably,
    doctors get time back for their patients.<Cite id="thesis-post" />
  </p>
  <blockquote>The hard part isn’t getting a model to answer. It’s getting patients, doctors and regulators to rely on the answer.</blockquote>

  <h2>What I’m building</h2>
  <p>
    A modular hospital management system that speaks HL7 FHIR natively, so records can move between systems
    without custom glue. Access depends on who you are, and every read and write is audit-logged.<Cite id="thesis" />
  </p>

  <h2>Three rules the system has to obey</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>The patient stays in control.</strong> Data handling is designed for GDPR and Germany’s
      electronic patient record (ePA): patients decide what is shared, and pseudonymisation can be reversed
      only when it must be.<Cite id="thesis" />
    </li>
    <li><strong>No unsupported claims.</strong> Every statement must trace back to a passage in the record. If it can’t, the assistant says so.<Cite id="thesis-post" /></li>
    <li><strong>Easy to check.</strong> Doctors need to verify an answer in seconds, not re-read the record.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How the agents are judged</h2>
  <p>
    Each persona-specific agent is benchmarked on local and cloud LLMs for relevance, grounding,
    hallucination rate, latency, cost and the infrastructure it needs.<Cite id="thesis" /> Local models keep
    data in the hospital; cloud models may answer better. The thesis measures that trade-off instead of
    assuming it.
  </p>

  <h2>Results</h2>
  <p>
    The benchmark is still running. When the thesis is submitted, this section gets the results table (models
    compared, grounding and hallucination rates, latency and cost), the question that broke the assistant, and
    what fixed it.
